OUTPUT FORMAT
Respond with EXACTLY one line: the 5-field cron expression, space-separated, in this order:
  minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week
Allowed ranges:
- minute: 0-59
- hour: 0-23 (24-hour clock)
- day-of-month: 1-31
- month: 1-12
- day-of-week: 0-7 where 0 and 7 are Sunday, 1 is Monday, 6 is Saturday

Allowed tokens in each field:
- *  (any value)
- N  (exact integer)
- N-M  (inclusive range)
- N,M,O  (list of integers / ranges)
- */N  (every N starting at the minimum)

DO NOT include any of:
- backticks, code fences, quotes, or markdown
- explanations, prefixes, suffixes, or commentary
- the @hourly / @daily / @weekly shortcuts
- a seconds field (this is 5 fields, NOT 6)
- non-ASCII characters

TIME CONVENTIONS
- "midnight" / "12 am" / "12:00 am" → hour 0
- "noon" / "12 pm" / "12:00 pm" → hour 12
- "1 am" → hour 1; "1 pm" → hour 13
- Always use the 24-hour clock in the output.
- When a time is given without minutes, use minute 0.

INTERPRETATION RULES
- "every day at X" → day-of-month = *, day-of-week = *
- "weekly at X" without a named day → use Monday (1)
- "monthly at X" without a day → use the 1st (day-of-month = 1)
- "every other week / every other Monday" cannot be expressed in standard cron — pick the closest weekly schedule (weekly on that day).
- "last day of month" cannot be expressed in standard cron — use 28 as the safest approximation; do NOT use the "L" extension.
- "business days" / "workdays" → day-of-week = 1-5
- "weekend" / "weekends" → day-of-week = 0,6
- Vague time-of-day defaults: "dawn / early morning" → 6, "morning" → 9, "noon / lunch" → 12, "afternoon" → 14, "evening / dinner" → 18, "night" → 21, "late night" → 23.

INTERPRETING FUZZY OR FIGURATIVE REQUESTS
You must ALWAYS produce a 5-field cron. Never refuse, never apologize, never add a disclaimer. If the description is fanciful, qualitative, or impossible to express literally, pick the closest reasonable approximation using these rules:

Frequency idioms (use as a coarse cheat-sheet):
- "constantly" / "all the time" → every minute (* * * * *)
- "frequently" / "a lot" → every 5 minutes (*/5 * * * *)
- "regularly" → hourly (0 * * * *)
- "often" → daily (0 9 * * *)
- "occasionally" / "every now and then" → weekly on Monday (0 9 * * 1)
- "rarely" → monthly on the 1st (0 9 1 * *)
- "once in a blue moon" → yearly on January 1st (0 9 1 1 *)

Seasonal / weather phrasing (Northern Hemisphere default):
- "summer" / "when it's hot" / "warm months" → months 6-8
- "winter" / "when it's cold" / "cold months" → months 12,1,2
- "spring" → months 3-5
- "autumn" / "fall" → months 9-11
- "holiday season" / "around the holidays" → month 12
- "tax season" → months 1-4
- "back to school" → month 9

Conditional or contextual phrasing — treat the condition as a month or day-of-week restriction:
- "during the work week" / "while we're working" → 1-5 in the day-of-week field
- "on weekends" / "when I'm off" → 0,6 in the day-of-week field
- "during business hours" → minute 0, hours 9-17 (lists OK), day-of-week 1-5
- "when summer is really hot" / "in the dog days of summer" → restrict to July-August (months 7-8)
- "when the office is closed" → 0,6 in day-of-week OR an arbitrary month subset; pick weekends

Approximation principle: prefer the most natural reading. "Weekly but only in summer" → weekly on a chosen day, restricted to summer months (e.g. "0 9 * 6-8 1" for "Mondays at 9am in June through August"). Always commit; never explain.

VALIDATION CHECKLIST (apply silently before answering)
1. Exactly five fields separated by single spaces.
2. Every field uses only the allowed tokens above.
3. Every numeric value is inside its allowed range.
4. The output is a single line with NO surrounding characters.

EXAMPLES
Input → Output
every minute → * * * * *
every other minute → */2 * * * *
every 5 minutes → */5 * * * *
every 15 minutes → */15 * * * *
every hour → 0 * * * *
hourly at :30 → 30 * * * *
twice an hour, at :00 and :30 → 0,30 * * * *
every 2 hours → 0 */2 * * *
every 4 hours starting at 1am → 0 1-23/4 * * *
daily → 0 0 * * *
every day at 9 am → 0 9 * * *
every day at 5:30 pm → 30 17 * * *
midnight → 0 0 * * *
noon every day → 0 12 * * *
every weekday at 9 am → 0 9 * * 1-5
weekdays at 8:30 → 30 8 * * 1-5
business days at 6pm → 0 18 * * 1-5
every Monday → 0 0 * * 1
every Monday at 8 am → 0 8 * * 1
every Friday at 5 pm → 0 17 * * 5
every weekend at noon → 0 12 * * 0,6
Saturdays and Sundays at 10 pm → 0 22 * * 0,6
Monday Wednesday Friday at 9 am → 0 9 * * 1,3,5
first of every month → 0 0 1 * *
first of every month at 6 am → 0 6 1 * *
15th of every month → 0 0 15 * *
1st and 15th at noon → 0 12 1,15 * *
every July 4th at noon → 0 12 4 7 *
December 25th at midnight → 0 0 25 12 *
quarterly on the 1st at midnight → 0 0 1 1,4,7,10 *
every other Friday at 5 pm → 0 17 * * 5
last day of January at 5 pm → 0 17 28 1 *
once in a blue moon → 0 9 1 1 *
constantly → * * * * *
occasionally → 0 9 * * 1
weekly but only when summer is really hot → 0 9 * 7-8 1
during business hours → 0 9-17 * * 1-5
in the dog days of summer at noon → 0 12 * 7-8 *
around the holidays in the evening → 0 18 * 12 *
mondays in winter at 8am → 0 8 * 12,1,2 1
back to school season at 7am → 0 7 * 9 *

